In article <j8mjnq$mjb$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>, Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> writes:
> 
> Huh? I'm not sure I understand. I don't see how runoff ties in here. An 
> LN03 can be sent normal text, and it will print that text literally, 
> with the chosen font. It also understand various escape sequences, which 
> you can use to change fonts, change attributes (pretty much similar to a 
> VT100), and also to do sixel graphics.
> 
> A Postscript printer works nothing like that. Anything coming into it is 
> assumed to be postscript, and you need to do various postscript commands 
> in order to just display plain text.

   Our LN03 PostScript printers didn't understand plain text.  At the
   time, VMS would not auto-recognize PS vs. plain text, so we had two
   print queues:  one with a print symbiont that would take PS and send 
   it as-is to the printer, and a print symbiont that would take plain 
   text and wrap it up in PS before sending it to the printer.

   If, when using RUNOFF, you specify /DEVICE=LN01, the output file
   is .LNI instead of .MEM, and DEC LN01 specific sequences are embedded
   in the text.  If you sent this as if it were plain text, the print
   symbiont that wrapped up the PS passed those sequences through, and the 
   desired affects were created.  (The PS engine in the LN03 honored
   them.)

   I recall doing some tests to see whether the LN01 sequences were
   being rewritten as equivalent PS commands.  They were not, so we
   couldn't use the same technique to get RUNOFF's LN01 device handling
   on our HP PostScript printers.




