There are so many things out there that are "perhaps" real.
Where does this constantly come from that everybody talks
about Alphas sure success if there had been just a little
more of marketing/CEO support/magic ingredient nr. 42?
When you look at the market today, you will see that a lot
of old architectures are gone the way of the Dodo bird.
Apple has chosen PPC to replace the old M68k, true.
But they have given up PPC again, not because it was slow,
but because it was apparantly impossible to get a fast
PPC chip in mobile devices. History shows that the Alpha
architecture suffers when it is implemented very cheaply.
(think Multia). That's not a role model for a Mac, not in
the huge notebook market of the 2000s, not in the 90s
and certainly not today when we almost don't care what
CPU powers our mobile phone.
Even for Intel it was difficult to turn around from Pentium4
to the newer-but-older Core architecture. Alpha would
have needed a facelift just like that.

That's at least what I think when I read stories like that.
I really liked Alpha, but looking back I don't see a chance
to survive for Alpha. Itanium will do no better. It can either
survive in it's small market or die, but it won't grow out
of there.

