If someone had told me thirty years ago that the dominant computer
archiecture of 2011 would be instruction-set compatible with the 8080,
I would have laughed at them.

Of all the high performance architectures to come out of the supermicro
era, none of them exist today on the desktop workstation, in spite of them
being far higher efficiency archiectures than the x86.

The reason the x86 is so fast is that huge amounts of money were spent
in making an inefficient archiecture fast.  A lot of that has to do with
Intel marketing.

But... even so... even Intel promoted some high performance systems like
the i860 and i960, which mostly flopped.  And there was always the iAPX432
which was a very high performance system (although it was heavily optimized
to reduce memory requirements which turned out to be a bad thing when memory
got very cheap).  Intel never knew what to do with any of these things.
Don't even get me started on the Itanium2.

Part of the success of the x86 was marketing, but part of it was just that
it developed such a huge installed base that it was worth putting money
into improving it, and that increased the installed base.  Rather like an
avalanche.

There are some vestiges of some of the high performance RISC systems left
in the embedded systems market, but for the most part all of the big ones
are dead and manufactures like AMD and Zilog either dropped their 29000
and Z-8000 architectures in favor of making x86 clones or concentrating on
the embedded systems market.

I think this is sad, because I think that a world where most systems all
have the same architecture actually discourages programmers from writing
good clean portable code, and that then makes it hard to move to other
non-dominant architectures.  There's that avalanche thing again.

>That's at least what I think when I read stories like that.
>I really liked Alpha, but looking back I don't see a chance
>to survive for Alpha. Itanium will do no better. It can either
>survive in it's small market or die, but it won't grow out
>of there.

This is true, and it's very sad.  And what is worse is that the sheer
cheapness of x86 systems today means there are fewer and fewer small
markets.

I am glad that I lived in the era when many different computer architectures
co-existed, and when a lot of research was being done on improving system
architecture.  I'm very glad I got to program in a huge variety of different
instruction sets, all with their own strangeness.  It's a shame that kids
today won't be able to have that experience, but by the same token... the
x86 today sure is damn fast.
--scott

