The Multia is among the slowest Alphas out there.
While 166 or 233MHz may sound pretty much okay,
the 21066 isn't a great performer and will be slower
than most 21064, no matter the clock rate. Maybe
those 100MHz machines will be slower...
The real problem with the Multia is the 2.5" SCSI disk.
I think the bigggest disk of that kind was about 1.2GB
and those are quite rare.
It really shows that this machine was meant to be a
simple client. Also the Multia is known to overheat.
Just about any other Alpha will make a more reasonable
VMS machine. Even a VAXstation 4000 will be more fun
to play with. Many years ago, I used a Multia running
Windows NT4. That wasn't so bad.
I have heard from people who run DEC Unix 3.2 or 4.0
on Multias. That seems to work too and it's much better
suited for the slow CPU.

> Well, this is the best I can do at the moment.  Unless someone donated
> a nice AlphaStation, or AlphaServer (e.g. DS10, DS15/A) to me.
> 
> Via the local UG/SIG I was promised a DS10, already in June, but that
> has not happened yet.

The DS10 is a good machine, but you don't need a box
like that to get a nice VMS system. I would count the
Personal Workstation (au series) as a reasonable minimum.

Dennis

