Hey, Scott!

As bad as this is in the microprocessor world, it's even worse in the universe
of the old minicomputer and mainfame systems.  The problem for researchers is
the same, though:  Things went by so quickly, replaced by "better" things which
in turn were also very quickly replaced, that much of the intervening history
disappeared unrecorded.  We are left to try to recover the past by examining
the ashes of the crumbs.

One of our goals at the museum has been to introduce modern students (middle/
high school as well as college) to vintage systems which provide a place where
they can grasp viscerally things like memory addressing and pointers.  Better
to light one wheatgrain lamp than to curse something?

